{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>From twenty years old, etc., whom you have grumbled.</b> It is necessary to understand the intent of His saying <b>whom you have grumbled</b>; for if He wished to say ״because you grumbled,״ the word ״because״ (עַל) is missing, and besides, there would have been no need to say it, since He had already said ״your grumblings, etc., I have heard״ (Numbers 14:27)." "<b>It appears</b> that the intent of the words is this: since HaShem said ״from twenty years old,״ He was concerned that they might understand the intent of the words to be everyone who is today twenty years old, and accordingly those who left Egypt under twenty would be included in the decree, since in the second year they reached twenty. Therefore He said <b>whom you grumbled</b> — meaning, from the time they began to grumble. And the scriptural measure is: those who grumbled against Me from twenty years old. Accordingly, it refers back to the first grumbling when they left Egypt — for had it been about their grumbling [at that later time], He would not have needed to be precise and say ״twenty years old״ at the time of the grumbling, since He is speaking at that same time which is the time they grumbled. And this is what He said above: ״and they have tested Me this ten times״ (Numbers 14:22) — thus you have learned that whoever was included in the twenty at the first sin is the one included in the decree." "Or perhaps, for this precise distinction was not sensed by that generation, and they understood the words according to their plain meaning — that the decree had come upon those who were then twenty years old. And therefore in the fortieth year, when they went out to dig on the night of the ninth of Av, not one of them died, and they checked on the tenth, the eleventh, the twelfth, the thirteenth, and the fourteenth, and made the fifteenth a day of rejoicing (Eikhah Rabbah, Prologue). Yet it is difficult: why did they think they would still die, after those who were twenty when they left Egypt were already known [to have died]? What further exposure did they still have to the decree? Rather, they thought that the decree had been upon those who were then twenty." "<b>Or perhaps,</b> HaShem too spoke His words in a balanced manner, following His custom of saying things that bear two paths: if they merit, it will be for good; if they do not merit, etc. And so you will find that when He said to man (Genesis 2:17) ״on the day you eat of it you shall surely die,״ He mentioned ״day״ indefinitely — you could say it means man's day, and you could say it means the day of the Holy One, blessed be He, which is a thousand years; and when man repented, HaShem inclined the intention toward merit, that it is the day of the Holy One, blessed be He. Likewise in the matter before us, the statement <b>whom you have grumbled</b> intended two paths: one — because you grumbled; and one — a designation for the beginning of the grumbling as we have said. If they merited, the matter would be interpreted as referring to the time of the Exodus, as we have said; and if they did not merit, the decree would be fulfilled also upon those who were under twenty when they left. And what He said, ״and they have tested Me this ten times,״ speaks of the majority of the grumblers; and therefore when they saw in the fortieth year that not one of them died, they made it a festival, because the decree had been annulled from those who left Egypt under twenty and were twenty years old at the time of the decree."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:31.905139180Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}